    {
     "t": "Brutus' death",
     "text": "Brutus escaped to the hills with about four legions' worth of men, but they would not try another battle. That night he killed himself, according to Plutarch with the help of his friend Strato of Aegeae. About 14,000 of his men surrendered and were enrolled in the triumvirs' armies. Plutarch says Antony covered the body with his own purple cloak. With Brutus and Cassius dead, organised republican resistance on land ended; Antony took the East, Octavian the task of settling veterans in Italy.",
