   "phases": [
    {
     "t": "The ditch",
     "text": "Belisarius, still under 30 and newly magister militum per Orientem, faced a Persian army that Procopius (Wars 1.13-14), his secretary and an eyewitness, puts at 40,000 and then 50,000, with about 25,000 Romans. He dug a ditch in front of Dara with several crossings: a central stretch set back, with cross-trenches at right angles at each end running forward to two straight wing ditches. The infantry stood behind the central ditch; the cavalry stood behind the wings; two bodies of 600 Huns waited in the angles, where they could strike sideways at any enemy who attacked either wing. On the first day there was only skirmishing and two single combats won by Andreas, a wrestling instructor from Byzantium.",
