     "text": "Narses, with an army of perhaps 20,000-30,000 including 5,500 Lombards and 3,000 Heruli, met Totila on the plain of Busta Gallorum in the Apennines. Procopius (Wars 8.31) describes his order: the Lombards, Heruli and other barbarians dismounted in the centre, so that they could not easily run away; Roman troops on the wings, with Narses and John on the left; 4,000 foot archers at each end of the line, angled forward in a crescent. On the far left, 1,500 horsemen: 500 to help any part of the line in trouble, 1,000 to get behind the Gothic infantry once it engaged. Before the battle 50 Roman infantry occupied a small hill on the left; Gothic cavalry charged them again and again, and the 50, packed shield to shield and rattling their weapons to frighten the horses, held.",
