   "phases": [
    {
     "t": "After days of fighting",
     "text": "In summer 636 a large Roman army, commanded by the Armenian Vahan with Theodore Trithyrios, Ghassanid and Armenian contingents, faced the Muslim forces that had withdrawn from Damascus and Emesa to the plateau east of the Wadi al-Ruqqad above the Yarmuk gorge. Numbers are unknowable: the Arabic tradition's 100,000-240,000 Romans are impossible, and modern estimates for each side range from 15,000 to 40,000. The armies had been facing each other for weeks, perhaps months, with sporadic fighting. The Arabic sources compress the battle into several days of hard combat in which Roman attacks almost broke the Muslim line and women at the Muslim camp drove fugitives back into the fight.",
